[[build-packets]]
=== Packet protocol

[abstract]
Every message between Morloc processes is a *packet*: a fixed 32-byte header,
optional metadata, and a payload. The same format carries calls, results,
errors and readiness pings over sockets, and it is also the on-disk format
of streams and cached results. This section specifies the header, each packet
type, metadata blocks, how a payload names its data, and the transport rules.

**Layout.** A packet is a header, then `offset` bytes of metadata, then
`length` bytes of payload. All integers are little-endian.

[cols="1,1,2,4", options="header"]
|===
| Offset | Size | Field | Meaning
| 0 | 4 | magic | `0x0707f86d` (bytes `6d f8 07 07`). A packet without it is rejected.
| 4 | 2 | plain | Reserved, 0.
| 6 | 2 | version | Reserved, 0.
| 8 | 2 | flavor | Reserved, 0.
| 10 | 2 | mode | Reserved, 0.
| 12 | 8 | command | Packet type (first byte) and type-specific fields.
| 20 | 4 | offset | Bytes of metadata between the header and the payload.
| 24 | 8 | length | Bytes of payload.
|===

**Packet types**, from the first command byte:

[cols="1,1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Type | Name | Purpose
| 0 | data | A value, or an error.
| 1 | call | A function call: which function, and its arguments.
| 2 | ping | Readiness check. The header alone; the reply is an identical ping.
| 3 | stream | Opens a stream file; the length field is all ones, since a stream has no fixed length (<<build-streaming>>).
| 4 | footer | Closes a stream file with its index (<<build-streaming>>).
|===

A **call** carries the function's index inside the receiving pool (the `mid`
from the manifest) as a 32-bit integer at byte 16, and an entry-point byte at
byte 13: 0 for an ordinary call, 1 for a call read from a file, as on a
cluster node. Its payload is the arguments, each a complete data packet,
concatenated in order. The receiver walks them by their own sizes.

A **data** packet describes its payload in the command field:

[cols="1,1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Byte | Field | Values
| 13 | source | 0 inline: the payload is the value. 1 file: the payload is a path. 2 pointer: the payload is an 8-byte relative pointer into shared memory (<<build-shm>>).
| 14 | format | 0 JSON, 1 MessagePack, 2 text, 3 packet (the file is itself a packet or stream), 4 voidstar (<<build-voidstar>>), 5 Arrow (<<build-tables>>).
| 15 | compression | 0 none, 1 Zstandard.
| 16 | encryption | Reserved, 0.
| 17 | status | 0 pass, 1 fail, 2 output pipe closed.
|===

Not every combination is meaningful. Values crossing between pools use
inline or pointer voidstar; tables use pointer Arrow; values in temporary
files (shared memory disabled) use file MessagePack.

**Errors** are data packets with status 1, inline source and text format;
the payload is the error message, including its cross-language traceback
(<<build-errors>>). Status 2 marks a write to a closed standard output, so
the nexus can end the run with the conventional broken-pipe status instead of
an error. A pool answers every call with exactly one packet: a request it
cannot even read is answered with an error packet rather than silence.

**Metadata** is a sequence of blocks. Each block is the three bytes `mmh`, a
one-byte kind, a 32-bit body size, then the body. Readers stop at the first
block that does not start with `mmh`, and the region is zero-padded to a
multiple of 32 bytes.

[cols="1,2,4", options="header"]
|===
| Kind | Name | Body
| 1 | schema | The schema string of the payload's type, NUL-terminated (<<build-voidstar>>).
| 3 | volume index | A 16-bit shared-memory volume index.
| 4 | frame index | Sizes of the payload's compressed frames; required when compression is Zstandard.
| 5 | subpacket index | Offsets and element counts of a stream's batches.
| 6, 7, 8 | stream diagnostics, final footer, footer status | Used only in stream files (<<build-streaming>>).
|===

A packet between pools needs no schema block, because both sides know the
type from the compiled program. Packets written to disk carry one, so that
any tool can read them without the program that wrote them.

**Transport.** Packets travel over Unix-domain stream sockets. Each call opens
one connection, sends one call packet, reads one reply packet, and closes.
There is no separate framing: the receiver reads the 32-byte header and then
exactly `offset + length` more bytes. Before a pointer payload is sent, the
sender takes a shared-memory reference on the receiver's behalf, so the
block cannot be freed in the gap before the receiver reads it; if the send
fails, the sender drops that reference again.

A caller waits for a reply as long as the callee lives, with no deadline: a
call may legitimately run for days. A pool reading a request waits as long as
the sending process exists, checking once a second.

